{{Warning sign}} while Philippine stamps are public domain as works of the Philippine government, one must also take into account the {{pg|Commons:Derivative works|underlying derivative works}} that may appear in the stamps, such as photographs from non-free sources and copyrighted artworks. Section 176.3 of the copyright law provides that the copyright in a work is not invalidated by its "publication or republication by the government in a public document." See also {{pg|Commons:Deletion requests/File:Catriona Gray 2019 stamp of the Philippines.jpg|this April 2020 deletion request}} and {{pg|Commons:Deletion requests/File:Pedro Calungsod 2012 stamp of the Philippines.jpg|this May 2021 deletion request}}.

==Threshold of originality== <!--T:94-->
</translate>
<noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|TOO|Philippines}}</noinclude>

*<translate><!--T:111--> {{OkMark}} for most utility models and industrial design works (as long as these do not feature artistic design and value). {{Unsure}}, assume {{NotMark}} in the case of logos.</translate>
<translate>
<!--T:112-->
With respect to the logos, the Philippine threshold of originality might be relatively low, for now. The Philippine Intellectual Property Office had accepted copyright registrations of some logos that might be simple under the {{pg|Commons:TOO US|the American jurisprudence or the US Copyright Office standards}}. It is possible that the ''sweat of the brow'' concept applies. Here are the examples (''title of the work &ndash; CCR number &ndash; issuance date &ndash; copyright holder'', as obtained from the [<tvar name="4">https://onlineservices.ipophil.gov.ph/CopyrightSearch/</tvar> copyright registry of  of the Philippines]):
</translate>
*[https://www.facebook.com/photo/?fbid=1440252059404713&set=a.461809919319626 Christian Society for the Deaf (CSD) Logo] &ndash; G-2019-00101 &ndash; {{date|2019|01|29}} &ndash; Iglesia ni Cristo
*[https://www.facebook.com/photo.php?fbid=772274671631805 Photo Sikwate Logo] &ndash; 2022-00957-G &ndash; {{date|2022|04|11}} &ndash; Roel S. Villaruel

<translate>
<!--T:113-->
The ''Intellectual Property Code'' states that the "literary and artistic works are original intellectual creations in the literary and artistic domain protected from the moment of their creation" and are protected "irrespective of their mode or form of expression, as well as of their content, quality and purpose."{{sref|8293/2015 Chapter VIII Section 172(1 and 2, portions)}} Nevertheless, one doctrinal passage used in [<tvar name="1">https://www.scribd.com/document/396856858/Copyright-Bar-q</tvar> Philippine Bar Exam questions] endorses an old US threshold of originality standard before the US courts significantly raised the bar of that standard after 1990:
</translate>
*<translate><!--T:114--> ''There will still be originality sufficient to warrant copyright protection if "the author, through his skill and effort, has contributed a distinguishable variation from the older works." In such a case, of course, only those parts which are new are protected by the new copyright.''</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:115-->
Philippine courts have also made rulings on ''other aspects'' of the originality matter:
</translate>
*<translate><!--T:116--> '''G.R. No. 161295''', ''Ching v. Salinas'' (2005): No copyright protection for utility models that have "no artistic design or value," even if having secured copyright registration. These cannot be classified as applied arts. In this case, it was an automotive bushing that the Supreme Court described as "comprising a generally cylindrical body having a co-axial bore that is centrally located and provided with a perpendicular flange on one of its ends and a cylindrical metal jacket surrounding the peripheral walls of said body, with the bushing made of plastic that is either polyvinyl chloride or polypropylene." In the context of useful articles, the court emphasized "the focus of copyright is the usefulness of the artistic design, and not its marketability." It added, "the central inquiry is whether the article is a work of art."<ref name=Ching2005/></translate>
*<translate><!--T:117--> '''G.R. No. 195835''', ''Olaño, et al. v. Lim'' (2016): "A hatch door, by its nature is an object of utility. It is defined as a small door, small gate or an opening that resembles a window equipped with an escape for use in case of fire or emergency. It is thus by nature, functional and utilitarian serving as egress access during emergency. It is not primarily an artistic creation but rather an object of utility designed to have aesthetic appeal. It is intrinsically a useful article, which, as a whole, is not eligible for copyright." The hatch doors made by LEC Steel Manufacturing Corporation had "no design elements that are physically and conceptually separable, independent and distinguishable from the hatch door itself." The court also found the panel of the door was "a mere reproduction of an already existing design."<ref name=Sison2016/></translate>

<translate>
<!--T:118-->
Until such time when either a definitive court casefile establishes the concrete threshold of originality on logos or the copyright office releases a strict compendium similar to Chapter 300 of the Compendium of U.S. Copyright Office Practices, the {{pg|Commons:Project scope/Precautionary principle|precautionary principle}} applies.
</translate>
 
